WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.500
- Why did the US go to war in Iraq?

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:04.263
- [Man] In the early
stages of evacuations.

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:08.870
- Growing up, the
narrative was always oil.

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:12.300
We went to Iraq to get oil,
like America always does.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:13.410
- We might find ourselves

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:15.460
without adequate supplies
of energy in the future.

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:17.780
- [Reporter] All major
oil fields in the south

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:19.810
are now under coaliltion control.

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:22.140
- But that's kind of the lazy explanation,

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:23.190
it's not quite it.

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:25.560
Oil is a part of it but
it's not the main reason.

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:27.170
We went to war in Iraq

00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:31.180
because several powerful
American men wanted to.

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:33.830
Some of them thought it
was vital, we had to.

00:00:33.830 --> 00:00:36.600
Others just wanted to
project American power.

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:39.050
But either way, here
we are 18 years later,

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:40.880
nearly 10,000 Americans dead,

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:43.430
over 200,000 Iraqis dead,

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:46.103
over a trillion tax dollars spent.

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:48.710
And the result?

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:51.300
A Middle East that is
less stable than ever

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:53.260
in part because of the US presence

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:54.840
and really because of it.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:56.460
How did this happen?

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:58.370
I mean, I grew up and lived through this.

00:00:58.370 --> 00:00:59.850
I studied it as an undergrad,

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:01.550
I studied it in grad school,

00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:05.760
and yet it's taken me until
now to really take a hard look

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:08.640
and to really understand
exactly what happened.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:11.790
So this is how the US stole Iraq.

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:14.270
- [Man] Saddam Hussein
is a tyrant, a murderer,

00:01:14.270 --> 00:01:16.350
and a man who has started two wars.

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:19.603
He's clearly someone who
cannot be trusted or believed.

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:24.660
- All right, so it's the night of 9/11,

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:26.610
the country is reeling.

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:29.370
Who did this terrible thing and why?

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:31.060
How should the US respond?

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:33.280
And it became clear very soon

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:36.040
amongst intelligence and
counter-terrorism people

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:37.660
that al-Qaeda was behind it,

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:41.073
this terrorist group that was
mainly based in Afghanistan.

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:45.390
Later that night, the night of 9/11,

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:46.470
over at the Pentagon,

00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:49.730
which earlier that day had
been hit by an airplane,

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:53.520
a memo comes in from the
Deputy Secretary of State.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:55.230
The memo is urgent.

00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:57.110
It's for the Defense Intelligence Agency,

00:01:57.110 --> 00:01:58.330
the people who've spent their time

00:01:58.330 --> 00:01:59.700
doing all the intelligence

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:02.390
for war and other defense purposes.

00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:04.640
And this memo tells
the intelligence agents

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:06.000
not to look into al-Qaeda,

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:07.620
the people they know did it,

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:09.910
but instead to look into ways

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:13.870
Iraq has been involved in
terrorism for the previous decade.

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:15.700
The intelligence agents who got this memo

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:17.697
sort of scratched their
head and were like,

00:02:17.697 --> 00:02:19.270
"Wait what? What's going on here?"

00:02:19.270 --> 00:02:21.590
These people were very
familiar with the intel

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:23.000
and they knew that this attack

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:25.470
had come from al-Qaeda in Afghanistan.

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:28.843
Afghanistan is more than 1,000
kilometers away from Iraq.

00:02:28.843 --> 00:02:30.410
It is a completely different place,

00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:31.390
they speak a different language,

00:02:31.390 --> 00:02:32.950
has nothing to do with Iraq.

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:34.900
Afghanistan and Iraq
are totally different.

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:37.540
We had just been attacked by al-Qaeda,

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:40.820
and yet the Deputy Secretary of Defense

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:43.750
is asking for information about Iraq

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:45.310
and its ties to terrorism.

00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:46.910
The person who sent this memo

00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:50.630
was the Deputy Secretary
of State Paul Wolfowitz,

00:02:50.630 --> 00:02:52.410
and he was just getting started.

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:54.993
(gentle music)

00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:57.950
A few days after 9/11,

00:02:57.950 --> 00:03:00.970
the president gathers
all of his top advisors

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:04.163
out at his forest retreat right
outside of Washington, DC.

00:03:05.270 --> 00:03:08.790
They were there to discuss how
to respond to these attacks.

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:12.170
The focus of this discussion
was, of course, Afghanistan.

00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:14.160
How do we retaliate against al-Qaeda,

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:16.160
this terrorist group that has been given

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:18.630
sort of a safe haven by the
government of Afghanistan?

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:21.110
So they're sitting in some
conference room in some lodge

00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:22.120
in this forest retreat

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:24.770
debating how they're gonna
retaliate against al-Qaeda,

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:28.430
and who pipes up again but
this guy, Paul Wolfowitz?

00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:30.470
This time with the support of his boss,

00:03:30.470 --> 00:03:32.320
Secretary of Defense Donald Rumsfeld.

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:33.987
So Wolfowitz pipes up and he's like,

00:03:33.987 --> 00:03:36.887
"You know, 9/11 was a
pretty sophisticated attack.

00:03:36.887 --> 00:03:40.007
"There's no way that this
ragtag terror group of al-Qaeda

00:03:40.007 --> 00:03:41.847
"could have pulled it off all alone.

00:03:41.847 --> 00:03:44.550
"They probably had help
from Saddam in Iraq."

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:45.820
And then Wolfowitz says

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:48.047
that "when it comes to global terrorism,

00:03:48.047 --> 00:03:51.100
"Saddam is actually
the head of the snake."

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:51.940
He actually said that,

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:55.350
basically saying that Saddam
is behind all of this.

00:03:55.350 --> 00:03:56.997
And everyone in the room is sort of like,

00:03:56.997 --> 00:04:00.377
"Um, what the hell is
this guy talking about?

00:04:00.377 --> 00:04:02.657
"Saddam Hussein is a really bad guy,

00:04:02.657 --> 00:04:04.737
"but he's not the head of the snake

00:04:04.737 --> 00:04:07.220
"of international terrorism."

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:08.053
All these people,

00:04:08.053 --> 00:04:12.600
these very informed international
advisors. (chuckles)

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:15.570
Oh man, after some astonished glances,

00:04:15.570 --> 00:04:17.680
the head of the Counterterrorism Center

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:20.177
raises his hands and says,

00:04:20.177 --> 00:04:22.007
"Mr. President," quote,

00:04:22.007 --> 00:04:25.117
"we were attacked on
9/11 by Osama Bin Laden

00:04:25.117 --> 00:04:27.597
"and al-Qaeda in Afghanistan.

00:04:27.597 --> 00:04:32.597
"Saddam Hussein and Iraq has
nothing to do with this."

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:35.300
It's just like, I'm sorry,

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:38.600
I feel like as an eighth
grader at this time

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:40.020
I was asking these questions.

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:42.170
I was like, "Is Iraq the
same as Afghanistan?"

00:04:42.170 --> 00:04:46.150
And here are the leaders of
our country having to explain

00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:48.450
that Saddam Hussein has nothing to do

00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:50.260
with Afghanistan and al-Qaeda.

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:51.700
So this meeting at Camp David,

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:54.650
they move on and they keep
talking about these plans

00:04:54.650 --> 00:04:56.010
to bomb al-Qaeda.

00:04:56.010 --> 00:05:00.160
But Paul Wolfowitz is stubborn
and he does not give up.

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:02.720
Later that day there's a
break in all of the meetings,

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:04.070
it's like the afternoon.

00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:07.020
Attorney General John
Ashcroft is playing the piano

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:08.520
in this sort of lobby

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:10.593
and a few others are singing along

00:05:10.593 --> 00:05:12.820
these piano spirituals that he's playing.

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:15.180
Condoleezza Rice is one of
these people singing along.

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:17.970
President Bush is
sitting over by the fire.

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:20.160
It's this rustic cabin, it's all cozy.

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:21.640
He's drinking a cup of coffee.

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:26.640
And who cozies up to him at
the fire but Paul Wolfowitz?

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:29.870
And Bush is like, "Uh, here we go again."

00:05:29.870 --> 00:05:31.847
So he's like, "Listen, check out my plan.

00:05:31.847 --> 00:05:34.837
"Half of Iraq's people
live either way up here

00:05:34.837 --> 00:05:35.937
"where they don't like Saddam.

00:05:35.937 --> 00:05:38.217
"They're Kurds, they've
been brutalized by Saddam,

00:05:38.217 --> 00:05:39.297
"they do not like him.

00:05:39.297 --> 00:05:41.917
"Or they live way down here in the south

00:05:41.917 --> 00:05:45.327
"which is right near the border
of our good friend Kuwait.

00:05:45.327 --> 00:05:47.337
"We could easily come into Iraq

00:05:47.337 --> 00:05:50.107
"and take both of these
big population centers.

00:05:50.107 --> 00:05:52.637
"Oh, and in doing so,"
says Paul Wolfowitz,

00:05:52.637 --> 00:05:56.237
"we could naturally take the
major oil fields in Iraq,

00:05:56.237 --> 00:05:59.930
"thus cutting off Saddam from
his main revenue source."

00:05:59.930 --> 00:06:00.763
Or, in other words,

00:06:00.763 --> 00:06:03.840
he said making Saddam, quote,
"the Mayor of Baghdad."

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:05.620
He would only have power in Baghdad,

00:06:05.620 --> 00:06:08.220
he would be cut off from the
north and south of his country.

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:10.910
In this moment I can almost
see George Bush's ears

00:06:10.910 --> 00:06:13.570
literally perking up when
he hears this last part

00:06:13.570 --> 00:06:15.880
about cutting off the oil supply.

00:06:15.880 --> 00:06:19.370
After all, Bush was a former Texas oilman

00:06:19.370 --> 00:06:22.090
and he knew the importance
of having access to oil.

00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:24.330
So this plan sort of intrigued Bush

00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:27.117
and he literally said to
Paul Wolfowitz, quote,

00:06:27.117 --> 00:06:29.310
"Why didn't you bring
this up in the meeting?"

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:30.143
And Wolfowitz is like,

00:06:30.143 --> 00:06:33.900
"Well, I didn't wanna step
on the toes of my superiors."

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:35.470
Wolfowitz, we know you just wanted

00:06:35.470 --> 00:06:36.820
to cozy up against the president

00:06:36.820 --> 00:06:40.995
and plant this seed of how it
would be to take over Iraq.

00:06:40.995 --> 00:06:43.390
(gentle music)

00:06:43.390 --> 00:06:46.680
So put yourself in George
Bush's shoes for a second.

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:50.230
Your country was just brutally
attacked on your watch,

00:06:50.230 --> 00:06:53.950
and you are completely
at a loss of what to do.

00:06:53.950 --> 00:06:56.910
There's no clear easy
target to retaliate against.

00:06:56.910 --> 00:06:58.240
After a day of meetings,

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:00.700
of sitting around trying to
figure out how to retaliate,

00:07:00.700 --> 00:07:02.510
George Bush is realizing

00:07:02.510 --> 00:07:06.130
that retaliating against
a tiny group of terrorists

00:07:06.130 --> 00:07:08.270
living in caves in Afghanistan

00:07:08.270 --> 00:07:11.740
is like trying to hit
smoke with a baseball bat.

00:07:11.740 --> 00:07:12.960
Kind of impossible.

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:15.980
What George Bush wanted
and felt like he needed,

00:07:15.980 --> 00:07:16.940
and it turns out

00:07:16.940 --> 00:07:19.730
what a lot of American
lawmakers also wanted,

00:07:19.730 --> 00:07:22.130
was a clear decisive target.

00:07:22.130 --> 00:07:24.290
Not to try to hit smoke
with a baseball bat,

00:07:24.290 --> 00:07:26.870
but a nice solid hit.

00:07:26.870 --> 00:07:28.200
A home run.

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:30.650
So I believe this day at Camp David,

00:07:30.650 --> 00:07:32.360
four days after 9/11,

00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:34.600
is when President Bush decided

00:07:34.600 --> 00:07:37.470
that he would find a way to invade Iraq,

00:07:37.470 --> 00:07:40.560
to make Americans and himself feel like

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:43.080
he was actually doing
something definitive,

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:44.670
like toppling dictators,

00:07:44.670 --> 00:07:48.340
as a rebuttal to the
terrible attacks of 9/11.

00:07:48.340 --> 00:07:50.310
Even though Iraq had
nothing to do with al-Qaeda,

00:07:50.310 --> 00:07:53.240
he and his people around
him would find a way

00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:54.705
to make a connection.

00:07:54.705 --> 00:07:56.480
(gentle music)

00:07:56.480 --> 00:07:57.800
- If they're harboring terrorists,

00:07:57.800 --> 00:07:58.740
why not go in and get them?

00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:01.280
- Well, the evidence is pretty conclusive

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:03.743
that the Iraqis have
indeed harbored terrorists.

00:08:06.380 --> 00:08:07.860
- So, over the next year,

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:11.440
this seed that Paul Wolfowitz
really planted and nourished

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:12.750
in George Bush's mind

00:08:12.750 --> 00:08:15.170
starts to spread to everyone around him,

00:08:15.170 --> 00:08:18.740
and they start to find a way
to sell the American public

00:08:18.740 --> 00:08:20.730
on this idea of war with Iraq.

00:08:20.730 --> 00:08:22.130
They get the British on board

00:08:22.130 --> 00:08:24.000
and soon there's a full-blown debate

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:25.770
which revolves around this story

00:08:25.770 --> 00:08:28.950
that Saddam has really
big terrible weapons

00:08:28.950 --> 00:08:31.860
and that he will likely give
those weapons to terrorists

00:08:31.860 --> 00:08:33.420
who will strike the United States

00:08:33.420 --> 00:08:36.800
in another 9/11-type attack but way worse.

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:39.180
- The debate about the war
against Iraq has divided many

00:08:39.180 --> 00:08:40.731
along the political spectrum.

00:08:40.731 --> 00:08:42.965
- Hundreds of millions of dollars

00:08:42.965 --> 00:08:44.767
and I think that that is unnecessary.

00:08:44.767 --> 00:08:47.420
- Real concern about this Russian war.

00:08:47.420 --> 00:08:49.220
- And Bush kept saying
weapons of mass destruction

00:08:49.220 --> 00:08:50.149
over and over again.

00:08:50.149 --> 00:08:51.710
He learned that short phrase.

00:08:51.710 --> 00:08:54.320
- Very dangerous weapons
of mass destruction

00:08:54.320 --> 00:08:55.890
into this country.

00:08:55.890 --> 00:08:57.870
- But wait, hold on a
second, don't be fooled.

00:08:57.870 --> 00:09:00.310
The lead-up to the Iraq
War is often remembered

00:09:00.310 --> 00:09:03.840
as this robust debate about WMDs,

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:04.970
weapons of mass destruction.

00:09:04.970 --> 00:09:08.500
- Saddam Hussein will continue
to increase his capacity

00:09:08.500 --> 00:09:11.780
to wage biological and chemical warfare

00:09:11.780 --> 00:09:15.260
and will keep trying to
develop nuclear weapons.

00:09:15.260 --> 00:09:16.670
- But I'm here to tell you

00:09:16.670 --> 00:09:18.230
that you need to think
differently about it.

00:09:18.230 --> 00:09:21.340
The decision to go to war
in Iraq was already made.

00:09:21.340 --> 00:09:23.660
It was made moments after 9/11

00:09:23.660 --> 00:09:25.490
when a small group of powerful men

00:09:25.490 --> 00:09:27.900
imagined a glorious military campaign

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:31.180
to liberate oppressed
Iraqis and topple a dictator

00:09:31.180 --> 00:09:33.280
to make themselves and their country

00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:35.620
feel like we got a home
run after we were attacked.

00:09:35.620 --> 00:09:39.240
From there, everything
molded to fit that decision.

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:41.410
Did Saddam Hussein have
weapons of mass destruction?

00:09:41.410 --> 00:09:43.070
Did he wanna give those to terrorists?

00:09:43.070 --> 00:09:45.000
No, there was no evidence.

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:46.160
The little evidence there was

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:48.620
was threadbare and super speculative.

00:09:48.620 --> 00:09:52.400
But these guys turned into
sort of like tea-leaf readers

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:54.130
where you can look at the tea leaves

00:09:54.130 --> 00:09:56.200
and see whatever you need to

00:09:56.200 --> 00:09:59.430
to support the decision that
they made many months earlier.

00:09:59.430 --> 00:10:00.810
And it wasn't just these guys.

00:10:00.810 --> 00:10:03.240
Soon, this vision of toppling Saddam

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:04.940
and having this American victory

00:10:04.940 --> 00:10:06.740
spread to the American Congress,

00:10:06.740 --> 00:10:10.100
Republicans and Democrats who
were also getting on board

00:10:10.100 --> 00:10:13.330
with this idea of
America, a force for good,

00:10:13.330 --> 00:10:14.580
toppling the bad guy.

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:18.600
It's a really wild study
in human psychology.

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:20.280
When you're a true believer in something,

00:10:20.280 --> 00:10:23.260
you really really believe
that you need to do something.

00:10:23.260 --> 00:10:27.150
Everything you see confirms
what you want to believe

00:10:27.150 --> 00:10:28.810
or what you need to believe.

00:10:28.810 --> 00:10:29.643
We all do it,

00:10:29.643 --> 00:10:32.730
but it's just scary when
our leaders are doing this,

00:10:32.730 --> 00:10:35.620
and they're doing it on
beliefs and decisions

00:10:35.620 --> 00:10:38.820
that have deep ramifications
for human suffering.

00:10:38.820 --> 00:10:41.510
So yeah, you can go over the
debates and the intelligence

00:10:41.510 --> 00:10:43.260
and the back and forth on whether or not

00:10:43.260 --> 00:10:45.010
Saddam had weapons of mass destruction.

00:10:45.010 --> 00:10:47.160
It was all a show to
help these people confirm

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:49.650
what they'd already
decided they wanted to do.

00:10:49.650 --> 00:10:50.750
So by the end,

00:10:50.750 --> 00:10:54.640
after weapons inspectors
had done 700 inspections

00:10:54.640 --> 00:10:55.690
and found no weapons,

00:10:55.690 --> 00:10:58.730
this group of guys eventually
just start resorting

00:10:58.730 --> 00:11:00.340
to full-on lying.

00:11:00.340 --> 00:11:03.210
Dick Cheney gets up in
his speech and says.

00:11:03.210 --> 00:11:05.130
- Simply stated, there is no doubt

00:11:06.347 --> 00:11:09.750
that Saddam Hussein now has
weapons of mass destruction.

00:11:09.750 --> 00:11:11.440
- Well, there was plenty of doubt.

00:11:11.440 --> 00:11:13.160
In fact, he simply stated,

00:11:13.160 --> 00:11:16.730
there can be no evidence
that Saddam has WMDs

00:11:16.730 --> 00:11:18.200
was the reality at the time.

00:11:18.200 --> 00:11:20.910
Even the Secretary of State, Colin Powell,

00:11:20.910 --> 00:11:24.320
who was totally against
this fantasy-filled vision

00:11:24.320 --> 00:11:26.160
of going and invading Iraq,

00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:28.120
even him eventually got on board,

00:11:28.120 --> 00:11:30.370
painstakingly getting in front of the UN

00:11:30.370 --> 00:11:34.760
and going over a bunch of
thin or non-existent evidence

00:11:34.760 --> 00:11:37.080
and claiming that it was watertight.

00:11:37.080 --> 00:11:39.850
- My colleagues, every
statement that I make today

00:11:39.850 --> 00:11:43.030
is backed up by sources, solid sources.

00:11:43.030 --> 00:11:44.760
These are not assertions.

00:11:44.760 --> 00:11:46.980
What we're giving you
are facts and conclusions

00:11:46.980 --> 00:11:49.290
based on solid intelligence.

00:11:49.290 --> 00:11:51.390
- Solid intelligence, no no no no.

00:11:51.390 --> 00:11:54.940
These are words that Colin
Powell has lived to regret

00:11:54.940 --> 00:11:56.549
for the rest of his life.

00:11:56.549 --> 00:11:57.692
A lot of people saw Colin Powell

00:11:57.692 --> 00:12:00.540
as the one sane guy in the room.

00:12:00.540 --> 00:12:02.680
And so here at the UN

00:12:02.680 --> 00:12:04.230
when he's drinking the Kool-Aid

00:12:04.230 --> 00:12:07.220
and backing this intelligence,

00:12:07.220 --> 00:12:08.470
a lot of people got on board.

00:12:08.470 --> 00:12:10.580
By October 10th, 2002,

00:12:10.580 --> 00:12:13.150
the US Congress was fully on board.

00:12:13.150 --> 00:12:15.620
Almost all the Republicans in the chamber

00:12:15.620 --> 00:12:17.250
as well as a sizeable group of Democrats

00:12:17.250 --> 00:12:21.250
voted to authorize the US
to use force against Iraq.

00:12:21.250 --> 00:12:23.220
- [Man] Senate Joint Resolution 45

00:12:23.220 --> 00:12:25.620
to authorize the use of
United States Armed Forces

00:12:25.620 --> 00:12:26.800
against Iraq.

00:12:26.800 --> 00:12:29.270
- He has said he wants the
power to be able to go to war.

00:12:29.270 --> 00:12:32.320
This seems completely
consistent with that request

00:12:32.320 --> 00:12:33.767
that we say, "Yes, Mr. President,

00:12:33.767 --> 00:12:35.070
"you have that power to go to war."

00:12:35.070 --> 00:12:39.140
- This resolution gives the
president the authority he needs

00:12:39.140 --> 00:12:41.910
to confront the threat posed by Iraq.

00:12:41.910 --> 00:12:44.480
- Saddam Hussein and his sons

00:12:44.480 --> 00:12:47.250
must leave Iraq within 48 hours.

00:12:47.250 --> 00:12:49.520
- And then it happened.

00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:51.030
- [Reporter] Sirens went off in Baghdad

00:12:51.030 --> 00:12:52.420
just a few minutes ago.

00:12:52.420 --> 00:12:55.290
An air raid is in progress over Baghdad.

00:12:55.290 --> 00:12:57.810
- Explosions in Baghdad.

00:12:57.810 --> 00:13:00.423
- [Bush] Our nation enters
this conflict reluctantly.

00:13:01.479 --> 00:13:03.274
- [Reporter] You could have a report

00:13:03.274 --> 00:13:07.254
that an estimated 30 Iraqis
surrendered at the border.

00:13:07.254 --> 00:13:10.004
(dramatic music)

00:13:15.277 --> 00:13:17.801
(jet whooshes)

00:13:17.801 --> 00:13:20.520
(triumphal music)

00:13:20.520 --> 00:13:22.690
- Two months after the invasion,

00:13:22.690 --> 00:13:25.860
George Bush gives a speech
on an aircraft carrier

00:13:25.860 --> 00:13:27.830
with this giant sign that says,

00:13:27.830 --> 00:13:30.030
quote, "Mission accomplished."

00:13:30.030 --> 00:13:32.950
This is the type of thing
Bush surely was dreaming about

00:13:32.950 --> 00:13:35.380
while he was sitting by
the fire at Camp David,

00:13:35.380 --> 00:13:36.700
drinking coffee,

00:13:36.700 --> 00:13:39.250
listening to Paul
Wolfowitz explain the plan

00:13:39.250 --> 00:13:40.390
to take over Iraq.

00:13:40.390 --> 00:13:43.740
Big triumphant America
just toppled the dictator.

00:13:43.740 --> 00:13:45.720
Mission accomplished.

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:47.530
But this is the part of the story

00:13:47.530 --> 00:13:50.160
when all of the dreams, the fantasies,

00:13:50.160 --> 00:13:53.470
the visions of glory start to topple.

00:13:53.470 --> 00:13:58.122
Mission accomplished was
really mission just beginning.

00:13:58.122 --> 00:14:00.705
(gentle music)

00:14:03.790 --> 00:14:05.890
One of the first things that the US did

00:14:05.890 --> 00:14:07.550
when they took over Baghdad

00:14:07.550 --> 00:14:10.650
was they dissolved the
huge Iraqi military.

00:14:10.650 --> 00:14:12.527
They just said, "No, we
don't want you anymore.

00:14:12.527 --> 00:14:16.087
"All of you commanders and
leaders, soldiers, you're out.

00:14:16.087 --> 00:14:17.350
"We're gonna start fresh."

00:14:17.350 --> 00:14:20.250
In typical simplistic thinking
of these visionary leaders,

00:14:20.250 --> 00:14:22.060
the idea was that doing
so would allow them

00:14:22.060 --> 00:14:24.500
to start totally fresh with a new army,

00:14:24.500 --> 00:14:28.380
completely rid of any sort of
influence of Saddam Hussein.

00:14:28.380 --> 00:14:32.040
So now you have a bunch
of ex-Iraqi army members

00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:35.010
who are angry at the United
States and unemployed.

00:14:35.010 --> 00:14:39.450
Oh, and all of them have
extensive military experience.

00:14:39.450 --> 00:14:42.450
- There is a large number
of former Iraqi soldiers

00:14:42.450 --> 00:14:44.280
that are unemployed now.

00:14:44.280 --> 00:14:46.150
That is a huge concern.

00:14:46.150 --> 00:14:48.750
- It is just painful.

00:14:48.750 --> 00:14:50.020
What a terrible decision.

00:14:50.020 --> 00:14:53.040
So these guys run off
and they start to join

00:14:53.040 --> 00:14:56.580
the growing number of
rebel groups that are there

00:14:56.580 --> 00:14:59.090
cropping up to oppose the
United States presence.

00:14:59.090 --> 00:15:02.180
One of those groups is
called al-Qaeda in Iraq,

00:15:02.180 --> 00:15:05.630
which later turns into
the Islamic State of Iraq,

00:15:05.630 --> 00:15:07.310
which eventually becomes

00:15:07.310 --> 00:15:11.500
